### FIDO2 Registration and Assertion HTTP Exchange — IS 7.3
### Lab: Day 17 — FIDO2 / Passkeys
###
### All credential values are PLACEHOLDERS — replace before use.
### Challenge values are base64url-encoded random bytes generated by IS 7.3 per ceremony.

###############################################################################
### PART 1: REGISTRATION CEREMONY
###############################################################################

### Step 1: Start Registration
# The client requests a new registration challenge from IS 7.3.
# IS 7.3 generates a cryptographic nonce (challenge) and returns credential creation options.
#
# QUESTION: What is the purpose of the `challenge` in this response?
# QUESTION: Why is `userVerification: required` specified here?
# QUESTION: What is `excludeCredentials` used for?

POST https://<PLACEHOLDER: is-host>/fido2/v2/registration/start
Content-Type: application/json
# Authorization: session cookie or Bearer token authenticating the user session

{
  "username": "<PLACEHOLDER: user@banking.example.com>",
  "appId": "<PLACEHOLDER: app-client-id>",
  "userVerification": "required",
  "authenticatorSelection": {
    "residentKey": "required",
    "requireResidentKey": true,
    "userVerification": "required",
    "authenticatorAttachment": "cross-platform"
  },
  "attestation": "direct"
}

###
### Expected response — HTTP 200 OK
### IS 7.3 issues a challenge and returns credential creation options.
###
### {
###   "requestId": "<PLACEHOLDER: server-request-id>",
###   "challenge": "<PLACEHOLDER: base64url-encoded 32-byte nonce>",
###   "rp": {
###     "id": "banking.example.com",
###     "name": "Example Bank"
###   },
###   "user": {
###     "id": "<PLACEHOLDER: base64url-encoded user-id>",
###     "name": "<PLACEHOLDER: user@banking.example.com>",
###     "displayName": "<PLACEHOLDER: User Display Name>"
###   },
###   "pubKeyCredParams": [
###     { "alg": -7,  "type": "public-key" },
###     { "alg": -257, "type": "public-key" }
###   ],
###   "excludeCredentials": [
###     { "id": "<PLACEHOLDER: existing-credential-id-if-any>", "type": "public-key" }
###   ],
###   "authenticatorSelection": {
###     "residentKey": "required",
###     "requireResidentKey": true,
###     "userVerification": "required"
###   },
###   "timeout": 60000,
###   "attestation": "direct"
### }
###
### NOTE: alg -7 = ES256 (ECDSA with P-256), alg -257 = RS256 (RSASSA-PKCS1-v1_5 with SHA-256)
### NOTE: `excludeCredentials` prevents registering a second credential from the same device.
###       If the authenticator already has a credential for this RP, it will return InvalidStateError.

###############################################################################

### Step 2: Complete Registration (Finish)
# After the browser calls navigator.credentials.create(), the authenticator returns an attestation.
# The client forwards the result to IS 7.3 to complete registration.
#
# QUESTION: What does `attestationObject` contain?
# QUESTION: What does `clientDataJSON` contain, and why does IS 7.3 check its `origin` field?
# QUESTION: After IS 7.3 verifies this request, what does it store?

POST https://<PLACEHOLDER: is-host>/fido2/v2/registration/finish
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "requestId": "<PLACEHOLDER: server-request-id-from-start-response>",
  "credential": {
    "id": "<PLACEHOLDER: base64url-encoded-credential-id>",
    "rawId": "<PLACEHOLDER: base64url-encoded-credential-id>",
    "response": {
      "attestationObject": "<PLACEHOLDER: base64url-encoded-CBOR-attestation-object>",
      "clientDataJSON": "<PLACEHOLDER: base64url-encoded-client-data-JSON>"
    },
    "type": "public-key",
    "clientExtensionResults": {}
  }
}

###
### Expected response — HTTP 200 OK
### IS 7.3 verifies the attestation and stores the credential.
###
### {
###   "message": "credential registration successful",
###   "credentialId": "<PLACEHOLDER: registered-credential-id>"
### }
###
### IS 7.3 stores:
### - credentialId (maps to this authenticator for this user)
### - public key (COSE-encoded EC or RSA key)
### - signCount (replay protection counter)
### - userHandle (links credential to IS 7.3 user account)
### - rpId ("banking.example.com")
###
### The private key NEVER leaves the authenticator.

###############################################################################
### PART 2: ASSERTION CEREMONY (Authentication)
###############################################################################

### Step 3: Start Assertion
# The client requests a fresh challenge for authentication.
# IS 7.3 returns the user's registered credential IDs so the authenticator knows which key to use.
#
# QUESTION: Why does IS 7.3 include `allowCredentials` in the response?
# QUESTION: What would change in this request for a resident key (passkey) flow?

POST https://<PLACEHOLDER: is-host>/fido2/v2/assertion/start
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "<PLACEHOLDER: user@banking.example.com>",
  "appId": "<PLACEHOLDER: app-client-id>",
  "userVerification": "required"
}

###
### Expected response — HTTP 200 OK
###
### {
###   "requestId": "<PLACEHOLDER: server-request-id>",
###   "challenge": "<PLACEHOLDER: fresh-base64url-encoded-nonce>",
###   "allowCredentials": [
###     {
###       "id": "<PLACEHOLDER: registered-credential-id>",
###       "type": "public-key",
###       "transports": ["usb", "nfc", "ble"]
###     }
###   ],
###   "userVerification": "required",
###   "timeout": 60000,
###   "rpId": "banking.example.com"
### }
###
### NOTE: Each call to assertion/start generates a NEW challenge. The old challenge is invalid.
### NOTE: For a resident key/passkey flow, `allowCredentials` would be [] (empty).

###############################################################################

### Step 4: Complete Assertion (Finish)
# The authenticator signed the challenge. The client forwards the signed response to IS 7.3.
#
# QUESTION: What does IS 7.3 verify in `signature`?
# QUESTION: What does IS 7.3 check about the `userVerification` flag in `authenticatorData`?
# QUESTION: Why does IS 7.3 track `signCount`?

POST https://<PLACEHOLDER: is-host>/fido2/v2/assertion/finish
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "requestId": "<PLACEHOLDER: server-request-id-from-start-response>",
  "credential": {
    "id": "<PLACEHOLDER: credential-id-from-allowCredentials>",
    "rawId": "<PLACEHOLDER: credential-id-from-allowCredentials>",
    "response": {
      "authenticatorData": "<PLACEHOLDER: base64url-encoded-authenticator-data>",
      "clientDataJSON": "<PLACEHOLDER: base64url-encoded-client-data-JSON>",
      "signature": "<PLACEHOLDER: base64url-encoded-signature>",
      "userHandle": "<PLACEHOLDER: base64url-encoded-user-handle>"
    },
    "type": "public-key",
    "clientExtensionResults": {}
  }
}

###
### Expected response — HTTP 200 OK
### IS 7.3 has verified the assertion. The authentication step is complete.
###
### {
###   "message": "assertion successful",
###   "username": "<PLACEHOLDER: user@banking.example.com>"
### }
###
### IS 7.3 verifies:
### 1. clientDataJSON.challenge matches the stored challenge (anti-replay)
### 2. clientDataJSON.origin matches RP origins list (anti-phishing)
### 3. rpIdHash in authenticatorData matches SHA-256("banking.example.com") (RP binding)
### 4. UP (User Present) flag is set in authenticatorData (user interacted)
### 5. UV (User Verified) flag is set — REQUIRED because userVerification: required (SCA!)
### 6. signature verifies against the stored public key for this credentialId
### 7. signCount > last stored signCount (detects cloned authenticators)
###
### On success, IS 7.3 completes the authentication step and issues an auth code (in a full flow).
